---
title: Data Flow
description: How data moves through the Lament system.
section: Architecture
order: 3
---

## Ingestion Flow

The scraper pipeline processes music in this order:

```
1. Search Spotify for track metadata
2. Match track to YouTube video
3. Download audio via yt-dlp
4. Convert to HLS segments (m3u8 + ts files)
5. Fetch cover art (Spotify / Deezer / iTunes)
6. Process images with sharp (resize, WebP)
7. Fetch lyrics from Genius
8. Upload audio segments to R2
9. Upload cover images to R2
10. Insert metadata into LibSQL
```

## Request Flow

When a user interacts with the Lament frontend:

```
Browser ──► SvelteKit Server Route (+server.ts)
                    │
                    │ Injects X-Master-Key header
                    │ Applies TTL caching
                    ▼
              Hono API (Cloudflare Workers)
                    │
            ┌───────┼───────┐
            ▼       ▼       ▼
         LibSQL   Cache    R2
         (data)  (LRU)  (files)
```

Key points:

- The browser **never** communicates directly with the Hono API.
- Credentials are injected server-side by SvelteKit.
- Public data endpoints have cache-control headers applied.

## Audio Streaming Flow

```
Browser requests track playback
        │
        ▼
SvelteKit proxy: GET /api/stream/:id/playlist.m3u8
        │
        ▼
Hono API: GET /stream/:id/playlist.m3u8
        │
        ▼
R2: Fetch m3u8 manifest
        │
        ▼
Browser (HLS.js) parses manifest
        │
        ▼
Sequential requests for .ts segments
```

HLS.js handles segment loading, buffering, and adaptive quality.
